  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "19fb22a6-0deb-42fb-888c-43e6636b550d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This self-paced lesson introduces students to building webpages in Web Lab by working with AI Tutor. **This variant is AI-first:** the code workspace is hidden by default on every level (students can opt in via the view toggle), and no level requires reading or writing HTML/CSS. The progression has three parts:\r\n\r\n- **Part 1:** Students communicate a vision in conversation and use AI Tutor to generate a first draft \\- quickly discovering that AI output doesn't always match what they had in mind.\r\n- **Part 2:** Students build the collaboration skills that close the gap: pointing precisely at what to change (in plain language), iterating one change at a time, brainstorming style directions before building, matching someone else's vision, and diagnosing two classic AI failure modes (over-interpretation and over-generation).\r\n- **Part 3:** Students return to their original page and iterate freely.\r\n\r\n**In order to get students started right away, the lesson has no slides and no dedicated warm up / wrap up** - instead, we recommend adapting any of your existing classroom routines to this lesson.",
        "name": "Lesson Overview"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19fb22a6-0deb-42fb-888c-43e6636b550d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4966791e-08bc-411f-b1e3-8aaedb5fdab7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1db1223a-ac7b-4172-9d73-a8a3df4c94f5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**Getting Started with Code.org:** Consider watching our [Getting Started with Code.org](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLzdnOPI1iJNdX7P76yGw7YUM4Csm1ZZmM) video series for an overview of how to navigate lesson plans, setup a classroom section, and other important features of the Code.org platform.\r\n\r\n> ⚠️ **Make sure AI Tutor is enabled:** AI Tutor is a required part of this lesson. If you plan on teaching it with your students, make sure you follow these steps:\r\n> 1) [Become a verified teacher](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/115001550131-How-to-Become-a-Verified-Teacher)\r\n> 2) [Assign the course to a section](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/202114633-Creating-Accounts-for-Students-and-Adding-Them-to-Your-Section)\r\n> 3) [Make sure AI Tools are enabled for that section](https://support.code.org/hc/en-us/articles/42550900593677-AI-Settings-Managing-Student-Access-to-AI-Tools)\r\n",
        "name": "Setup the Classroom"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1db1223a-ac7b-4172-9d73-a8a3df4c94f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4966791e-08bc-411f-b1e3-8aaedb5fdab7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c86219a5-4a4d-4379-89fa-2c85c9825019",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We recommend attempting each level before running this activity for students. Note that in this variant the code workspace is hidden by default on every Web Lab level - students (and you) can reveal it with the Code/Split view toggle at the top of the workspace, but nothing in the lesson requires it.",
        "name": "Preview the Levels"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c86219a5-4a4d-4379-89fa-2c85c9825019",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4966791e-08bc-411f-b1e3-8aaedb5fdab7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fe26717-d537-4852-a44c-4c0c14a6c7f8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Students build something real, fast, entirely through conversation with AI Tutor. Expect 15-25 minutes.\r\n\r\n| Level | Title | Description |\r\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\r\n| 1 | Introduction to Part 1 | Slideshow: **designers communicate a vision**. |\r\n| 2 | Meet Your AI Collaborator | Students make their first change to a practice otter page: ask in their own words, judge the preview, Accept/Reject. |\r\n| 3 | Tell AI Your Vision | Students compose a complete vision (about, includes, tone, colors, why) as a chat message and get a first draft. |\r\n| 4 | Reflection: Your First Version | Students reflect on the vision/result gap and are prompted to fact-check the content AI wrote. |"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fe26717-d537-4852-a44c-4c0c14a6c7f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bad51944-8a91-40d2-9a1c-c0e0ad24ee34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f761b16b-f8c2-420f-ad3b-a3beb427bc80",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 1: Establish a Vision",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "**Staying in Early Levels:** The goal of Part 1 is a first draft, not a finished page. If students linger because they feel \"stuck\", encourage them onward with the promise that Part 2 teaches them how to get unstuck."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f761b16b-f8c2-420f-ad3b-a3beb427bc80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bad51944-8a91-40d2-9a1c-c0e0ad24ee34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9324f85d-1456-45b1-b18b-c52811d91809",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Students build AI-collaboration skills, one per level. Each level's AI is configured to reinforce the skill (asking clarifying questions when the target is vague, making one change per request, describing ideas before building, acting only on explicit requests). Expect 25-35 minutes.\r\n\r\n| Level | Title | Description |\r\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\r\n| 5 | Introduction to Part 2 | Slideshow: essential question - **How do designers close the gap between vision and reality?** |\r\n| 6 | Point at What You Mean | Precision without jargon: name which part of the page (\"the heading that says...\") plus the outcome. Vague targets get a clarifying question instead of a guess. |\r\n| 7 | One Change at a Time | Iteration granularity: three separate requests, each judged before the next. The AI makes exactly one change per response. |\r\n| 8 | Brainstorm Before You Build | AI as ideation partner: ask for three style directions in words, pick one, then apply it. |\r\n| 9 | Prompting Puzzle: Jordan's Garden | Match someone else's non-technical vision and screenshot. The AI acts only on explicit student requests. |\r\n| 10 | Debug the Gap: Maya's Page | Over-interpretation: \"colorful\" became a rainbow explosion. Students restate the vision precisely. |\r\n| 11 | Debug the Gap: Carlos's Page | Over-generation: AI added recipes nobody asked for. Students practice negative constraints - saying what *not* to do. |\r\n| 12 | Reflection: What Changed? | Check for Understanding. |"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9324f85d-1456-45b1-b18b-c52811d91809",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edd5c76e-ec1e-487b-8841-1691152ac7a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a226376-b1e0-4d17-9191-1e07213a9407",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 2: Close the Gap"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a226376-b1e0-4d17-9191-1e07213a9407",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edd5c76e-ec1e-487b-8841-1691152ac7a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50cb9612-8365-45fb-bb86-53d5d07c2812",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for responses that name a specific collaboration move - pointing at a target, working in smaller steps, asking for ideas first, or saying what *not* to do - rather than generic \"be more specific\"."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50cb9612-8365-45fb-bb86-53d5d07c2812",
        "lesson_activity.key": "edd5c76e-ec1e-487b-8841-1691152ac7a4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5cabcb7f-9c56-4b92-b53a-8081a6b02922",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Students return to their own page from Part 1 and iterate freely, with a reminder to fact-check AI-written content. Expect the remainder of class.\r\n\r\n| Level | Title | Description |\r\n| :---- | :---- | :---- |\r\n| 13 | Introduction to Part 3 | Slideshow: three ways of working with AI - detailed vision (Dev), step-by-step co-building (Mia), careful judging (Jordan). |\r\n| 14 | Revisiting Your Vision | Students reload their page from Level 3 with full choice about how to continue. Share and Remix available. |"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5cabcb7f-9c56-4b92-b53a-8081a6b02922",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c707b6c1-b986-4718-b34e-33f9af695cca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cdc21e22-53bf-49f6-b3ab-f4131aa0dbb4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Part 3: Iterate for Real"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cdc21e22-53bf-49f6-b3ab-f4131aa0dbb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c707b6c1-b986-4718-b34e-33f9af695cca"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2402541a-9529-4b0a-b41e-1013923ba145",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "**More Projects in Web Lab:** Web Lab is a tool that can be used for standalone projects outside of this lesson. You can find Web Lab in the \"New projects+\" menu in the header. [Click here to learn more about Web Lab and standalone projects](https://code.org/en-US/tools/web-lab).\r\n\r\n**Learn More about Web Development and AI:** If your students want to see and write the code behind their pages - HTML, CSS, and eventually Javascript - consider exploring our [AI Foundations Semester 2 course](https://code.org/en-US/incubator).",
        "name": "What Comes Next?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2402541a-9529-4b0a-b41e-1013923ba145",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b37bf795-bdb6-46d1-9eb3-6f37644c5a99"
      }
    }
  ],
